[Music]  tech companies do way more than tech  these days  they're now an indispensable part of our  everyday lives  from how we communicate to how we shop  and pay for things  many have grown quickly in less than two  decades and that's especially true in  china  alibaba is the biggest e-commerce  company in the country  by most measures and runs a system  called alipay which is this  phenomenally important payment platform  last year more than 700 million people  used alibaba's platforms to buy things  online  and more than a billion people use the  fintech giant ant group's mobile app  to make payments borrow money buy  insurance and invest  we just depress buy alibaba was founded  by chinese entrepreneur jack ma  and he's ant's controlling shareholder  the two companies helped steer the  country into the digital marketplace  and pushed it to become an increasingly  cashless society  their impact on the economy has drawn  the attention of chinese regulators who  like their counterparts in the u.s  have been trying to rein in big tech  there isn't much really that governments  in washington and beijing can all agree  on  but at the moment push is underway to  curb the power  of the world's biggest tech giants china  appears to be moving on a faster time  frame for much of this than the us  here's how china was able to bring jack  moss companies in line  in just a few months the main driver of  why china can move fast is because of  its political system  china is a highly centralized one-party  state  and so that means in turn that when the  chinese authorities do  decide to do something they can  sometimes move much faster  and that type of speed when it comes to  regulatory action was on full display  with alibaba and ant  it all started with this speech by jack  ma in october of last year  in this speech jack mark effectively  criticized the people who are  responsible for overseeing the chinese  economy and for overseeing his  companies  and as the wall street journal has  reported that  infuriated china's top leaders and in  fact  president xi jinping himself made the  decision  my colleagues have reported to scupper  the pending  ipo event as a result partly of  the speech ant was supposed to list on  thursday  and not anymore what would have been the  world's largest ipo  was called off less than two weeks after  that speech that kind of direct  intervention by a national level  leader in what is effectively a kind of  commercial process  it's not like you would necessarily see  something equivalent happening  in the u.s about two months later  chinese regulators came down hard on  moz2 companies  they criticized ant's behavior toward  consumers and rivals  and told it to refocus on its payments  business and fix problems in newer  ventures  regulators also launched an anti-trust  investigation into alibaba  saying the company abused its dominant  market position forcing some sellers to  operate only on its platforms  alibaba didn't comment on the  allegations while ann said it  appreciated the guidance  that probe lasted less than four months  and alibaba was hit with a 2.8 billion  dollar antitrust fine  while ant will soon be directly overseen  by china's central bank  so for mos speech to penalties  regulators made it all happen  in under six months in the us you might  expect some of these things to last  many years just take a look at four  american big tech giants that have been  accused of anti-competitive behavior in  the digital marketplace  the testimony you're out to give is true  and correct to the best of your  knowledge  information and belief so help you god  while the usually visible jack ma  disappeared after the ipo was scrapped  in the u.s the ceos were called to a  very public hearing in front of house  lawmakers  you had four of the most powerful  technology executives in the world  subject to hours of hostile questioning  which came from both sides of the aisle  this hearing came amid a congressional  fact-finding investigation  that had been underway for more than a  year remember chinese regulators  anti-trust investigation into alibaba  from start to finish  took about four months at the end of the  house democrats 16-month probe  lawmakers concluded that the four  companies had leveraged their dominance  to stamp out competition  and stifle innovation republicans said  they supported strong antitrust  enforcement targeting the companies  the four companies disputed the findings  since then there have been a series of  blockbuster lawsuits launched by u.s  federal agencies and state governments  aimed at enforcing existing antitrust  rules but as  of april u.s regulators and lawmakers  haven't ordered these companies to  reform their business practices  the u.s is of course a democracy with  all of the checks and balances  and requirements for transparency that  that involves  and that means that companies can push  back for example the popular  chinese-owned video sharing app  tick-tock we're looking at tick-tock we  may be banning tick-tock  in august last year the trump  administration signed an executive order  that could effectively ban americans  from using and downloading the app  this was over concerns that user data  could be shared with the chinese  government  ticktock said that the chinese  government has never asked for user data  and would refuse such a request within  the u.s legal system the company  fought back tiktok filed lawsuits  against the order and less than two  months later  a federal judge blocked the  administration's van but for jack ma and  his companies  there was no room for appeal chinese  political context basically it makes it  much harder for companies  or others to push back if they disagree  alibaba immediately accepted the fine  and said it would invest in measures to  support merchants on its platforms  while ann said it would maintain the  continuity and quality of its services  while it fully complies with regulations  this regulatory assault has also gone  beyond jack moss companies  a few days later nearly three dozen  other big tech companies in china also  vowed to comply with the country's  anti-monopoly regulations  so while these two companies have been  shining examples of chinese innovation  from beijing's perspective their success  needs to align with the party's  interests  if there was a sense in which it was  allowing the private sector to  command bits of the economy it is now  kind of pushing alibaba and other  companies to fall  more tightly in line with its own  priorities  it's about kind of making sure that  government control or  party control over the chinese economy  is secure

